# Intent: Games item 2, the device player and its tools (builds on G01). maipai_games.cc draws a running game with LVGL objects on the top layer (never clickable, so a touch still reaches the face's own handlers, which pass it on in patch G03), runs the player from one LVGL timer every 30 ms that exists only while a game runs, plays the tones the player queues through the sound policy (maipai_sounds_play: the policy, night rule and hard mute decide, a refusal is silence), keeps the unit awake while a game runs, saves a finished game's score once into its own NVS namespace mp_games (five u16 per game, written on the main task, never on the screen task, and only for a game that reached its end), and ends the game on a stop request, on full quiet, or when an update starts. A start is refused with a plain sentence in full quiet, during an update, while another game plays, for an unknown name. Tools: self.game.list (games, titles in the saved language, best five scores, whether one is playing), self.game.start (name: memory, reaction or whack, or a title), self.game.stop, self.game.scores. Memory: while no game runs nothing exists (no task, no timer, no objects, no PSRAM; the packs are read in place from flash); a running game holds one Game record of about 0.5 KB in PSRAM (492 bytes measured) and its LVGL objects (about 2.4 KB of internal RAM for memory, 4.1 KB for whack-a-mole, freed when it ends). A bench build adds self.game.bench_tap (a finger at x, y; the reserved edges do nothing, as for a finger), self.game.bench_request_stop (the stop word path), self.game.status and self.game.bench_install (one outside pack, in pieces, through the same checks: size, checksum, tables, signing rule, a name no built-in uses).
# Placement: unit (the screen, the touches, the tones and the scores belong to the robot)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_decor.cc (the shape of a registered feature with an LVGL timer) and update_verify.cc of patch 155 (the Ed25519 check, reused for outside packs); the rest is new MaiPai code
# Check after a flash (bench build): self.game.list names three games; self.game.start memory answers ok and self.game.status shows active true, then a bot taps the shown colours with self.game.bench_tap and the score counter rises; a wrong colour ends the game on its end screen and self.game.scores shows the score; self.game.stop ends a game and saves nothing; self.game.bench_request_stop ends one the way the stop word does; self.privacy.set quiet 1 ends a running game and a start is refused with the full quiet sentence; scores are still there after a restart; self.game.bench_install refuses a damaged pack and a built-in name in plain words and accepts an unsigned pack with a new name.
# Kconfig: MAIPAI_GAMES (G01); off removes the player and the tools. MAIPAI_GAMES_BENCH adds the bench tools.
